		== Formal language notes

		- 2020\. Context-free languages: A constructive characterization.\
		  #paperlink(<paper-cf-grammars>) #bracketlink("GitHub", "https://github.com/KenyC/CF")\
		  #collapsible[
			In this note, I propose a constructive characterization of the class of context-free languages. The goal is to characterize it as the smallest class of languages closed under natural operations, analogous to the characterization of rational languages. The proposal uses union, concatenation, lambda-abstraction, and fixed points, and is illustrated with Haskell code.
		  ]
		- 2017\. Universal languages.\
		  #paperlink(<paper-universal-languages>)\
		  #collapsible[
			This note distinguishes two views of Universal Grammar: one in which it specifies a class of languages, and one in which it specifies a single formal language whose interface mappings produce different languages. It argues that the latter view is compatible with some classes, such as context-free languages, but not with others, such as mildly context-sensitive languages.
		  ]

